Marc A. Pelletier wrote on 14 Aug 2004 23:32:06 -0700 in article 
news:<920702ec.0408142232.2046d903@posting.google.com>:
> As I smell the sweet scent of approaching tournaments (brimstone,
> sweat and napalm), I decided to implement one of my old ideas:  fix
> scoring a bit so that it rewards play and not repetition.

This is long over-due.  What is the purpose of the score?  To compare games:

1.  Some ascensions are better than others.  The current score system fails 
utterly at this comparison.
2.  Some non-ascensions are better than other non-ascensions.  The current 
score system does an OK job at allowing newbie players compete with each 
other, but it could be improved.
3.  An ascension should always be worth more than a non-ascension.  The 
current score system fails here.

> This current revision does not implement feats (lists of things both
> generic and role-related you can do to increase your peril and your
> score.  Things like eating a cockatrice,

After reading over your patch spoiler, I have to say that I don't like it.

How would I do it?  The score for ascensions should be based on turns used 
and conducts.  If something is truly skill testing (naked, Zen), it should be 
added to the game as a conduct; otherwise ignore it.  Ascending with one of 
each gem, extincting a race, etc, are fun tricks, but meaningless.

Score for non-ascensions should be based on progress made, which comes down 
to levels visited, levels gained (but only up to 14), and bonuses for 
achievements like beating Sokoban, reaching Minetown, embarking on the quest, 
collecting the castle wand, and so on.  Conducts shouldn't count.

> Something that almost certainly will need to change:  the score is, at
> one point, scaled by difficulty of the role you play.  That difficulty
> is subjective and biased by my own difficulty with the various roles. 
> I'd appreciate input on what is currently implemented:

The problem with scaling score by role is it can make the bad roles better 
than the good ones, especially for ascensions.  If there's going to be 
scaling, it should be slight.  

